WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.929 --> 00:00:15.459
AUDIENCE MEMBER: I was wondering if

00:00:15.459 --> 00:00:19.480
either of you, whether you could compare the
French attitude towards Muslim immigrants

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:23.589
with the attitude in the US? Because it seems like US
distrust 

00:00:23.589 --> 00:00:28.230
of Muslim communities here is fueled more
by fears of terrorism than anything, in that

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.410
the religious aspects are more a side

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:36.420
effect of, like-- it's not a direct assault
on, like, the veil or something like that.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:40.030
And so I was wondering why that might be. Is it 
because 

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:44.610
the US is generally a more religious country
and so is less inclined to distrust religious practices

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:48.660
just because they are religious practices?
Or is there a certain different organization of

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:50.370
Muslim communities here that makes them

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:56.610
more willing to integrate, I suppose, into
American communal life?
GORSKI: I mean I think,

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.160
you know, Ates

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.000
kind of outlined the

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.550
usual and also, I think, correct answer
to that question, which is,

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:06.520
it has to do with the very different political

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:09.510
histories, and therefore very different political
cultures,

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:14.100
of France and the United States. In
particular, that

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.470
the dominance, the predominance,

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:21.730
of particular-- what I would call radical or
secularist republicanism,

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:25.360
you know, with its origins in the French
Revolution.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.700
THe US also has been

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:32.840
deeply influenced by republicanism, but
it was a different kind of republicanism--

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:36.790
a more deistic republicanism which never
took the kind of radically

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:38.190
anti-clericalist bent

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:40.409
that the French Revolution did, and

00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:41.670
there has always-- there--

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.520
it--

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:49.640
there is, you know, certainly from kind of
the early republic on, increasingly 

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:54.880
a kind of laissez-faire liberalism 
which emerges, which

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.219
basically understands religion as a form
of self--

00:01:58.219 --> 00:02:01.000
as a-- as, you know, a right,

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.990
sort of falling into the rights of conscience,
and as a form of self-expression.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:10.569
And that's been reinforced historically, of course,
through the arrival and absorption-- and

00:02:10.569 --> 00:02:15.809
emergence of more and more-- more and more
religious groups.

00:02:15.809 --> 00:02:19.339
To answer the other question, though, about--
and I don't know, maybe Ates is going

00:02:19.339 --> 00:02:23.899
to disagree with me about this-- I don't think
that you can pull apart the headscarf

00:02:23.899 --> 00:02:25.329
debate and--

00:02:25.329 --> 00:02:27.989
and 9/11 and other-- other

00:02:27.989 --> 00:02:33.019
encounters like that. So, for example, one 
of my students last year wrote a thesis

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:37.449
on British and French discourse around
the headscarf debate, and

00:02:37.449 --> 00:02:39.219
there is a

00:02:39.219 --> 00:02:40.229
remarkable shift

00:02:40.229 --> 00:02:42.799
within Britain, after

00:02:42.799 --> 00:02:44.989
9/11,

00:02:44.989 --> 00:02:49.569
away from multiculturalism and towards something,
actually, that increasingly looks like

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:52.269
French republicanism, and a similar

00:02:52.269 --> 00:02:53.599
shift can be seen

00:02:53.599 --> 00:02:58.329
in the Netherlands. So there, the kind
of-- the catalyst for that is the Theo Van Gogh 

00:02:58.329 --> 00:03:00.419
murder.

00:03:00.419 --> 00:03:02.900
But, you know, a similar kind of backlash. 

